        <p>Two rows in that table cause most of the confusion. First, biweekly and semi-monthly are not the same check. Biweekly is 26 paychecks of $4,400.00, semi-monthly is 24 paychecks of $4,766.67, and the two extra biweekly checks a year are what make the totals equal. A month with three biweekly checks pays $13,200.00 gross ($10,305.06 take-home) against a normal two-check month of $8,800.00 ($6,870.04 take-home), which is why biweekly pay feels lumpy. Second, the monthly average divides by 12, not by 4: four weeks is only $8,800.00, and the $733.33 difference is the extra 1.33 weeks the average month actually holds. Our <a href="/articles/how-many-paychecks-in-a-year/">paychecks-per-year guide</a> maps the three-paycheck months for every 2026 cycle, and our <a href="/articles/biweekly-vs-semi-monthly-pay-difference/">biweekly-versus-semimonthly comparison</a> runs the two schedules through a full year on the same salary.</p>
