        <p>Read the last column top to bottom, because the pattern is the point of this section: <strong>the fewer hours you work, the more each hour keeps</strong>. That’s $46.29 at 20 hours a week against $42.94 at 40, a $3.35-an-hour gap on the same $55.00 rate. The reason is the bracket. None of a 20-hour year’s $41,100.00 of taxable income reaches the 22% bracket, while a 25-hour year’s $55,400.00 of taxable income runs $5,000.00 into it, so the 25-hour row is the first schedule on this page where the 22% bracket shows up at all. Three things in that table matter more than the headline. First, the 35-hour week crosses $100,000 gross on its own, at $100,100.00, and its $79,250.35 of take-home already beats a $100,000 salary by $70.35, before overtime or a raise. Second, the 37.5-hour paid week, common in offices that pay for a 7.5-hour day, is $107,250.00 gross, which still beats a $105,000 salary by $2,250.00 gross and $1,582.87 of take-home. A 2.5-hour shorter week costs $7,150.00 gross and $5,030.03 of take-home. Third, the step from 30 to 40 hours is worth $28,600.00 gross and $20,120.10 of take-home ($1,676.68 a month). That is the single biggest lever on this page, bigger than any raise in the nearby-rates table. Part-time keeps a bigger share: at 20 hours you keep 84.2% of gross, against 78.1% full-time, and a 20-hour week at $55 beats a full-time $27-an-hour job by $835.64, while a 25-hour week beats a $70,000 salary’s take-home by $1,055.25. Our <a href="/articles/how-to-convert-part-time-hourly-pay-to-annual-salary/">part-time conversion guide</a> works the same ladder for any rate and any hours, and our <a href="/articles/how-many-hours-is-full-time/">full-time-hours guide</a> explains why 30 hours is the line where benefits usually start.</p>
