      <section class="article-section" id="limitations">
        <h2>Limitations</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Federal only unless stated:</strong> the take-home sketch uses 2026 federal rules for a single filer taking the $16,100 standard deduction. State and local taxes are excluded, so your actual take-home is lower in most states.</li>
          <li><strong>Full-time year assumed:</strong> the $114,400.00 headline assumes 40 paid hours in all 52 weeks. Part-time hours, unpaid weeks, and seasonal gaps lower it, and the schedules and unpaid-weeks tables price exactly how much.</li>
          <li><strong>Overtime is not guaranteed:</strong> the overtime sections assume those extra hours are actually there and paid at time and a half. Exempt workers get no overtime premium, and some employers cap overtime hours.</li>
          <li><strong>The 22% bracket slice is exact for this rate only:</strong> the $47,900.00 figure depends on $114,400.00 of gross and the $16,100 standard deduction. A 401(k) contribution, a different filing status, or a second income changes where the bracket line falls. The 24% bracket starts at $105,700.00 of taxable income, or about $58.56 an hour on straight time, though five overtime hours a week already reach it at $55 an hour.</li>
          <li><strong>Benefits are priced separately:</strong> the salary comparisons exclude health premiums, 401(k) matches, and paid time off unless we say otherwise in a note. A strong benefits package can flip the $110,000 row.</li>
          <li><strong>Rounding:</strong> per-check figures are rounded to the cent, so multiplying a rounded monthly figure by 12 can differ from the annual figure by a few cents.</li>
          <li><strong>Not tax or legal advice:</strong> these figures are 2026 federal estimates for a single filer taking the standard deduction, using the same method as the site’s other guides. Your employer’s payroll calendar, withholding elections, and benefit deductions change the figures.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

